task:
Prepare a clean, repository-owned fresh self-hosted Octopus deployment bundle for the new ED-Finder server. Start from exact origin/main `fd64ef8e14b5b026cec2d14949115ab681fb0b7d`. This is intentionally a fresh installation, not a migration: do not read, copy, export, or depend on the old `/opt/octopus` PostgreSQL/Qdrant data. Historical Octopus comments already posted to GitHub remain there; internal Octopus accounts, indexes, usage history, and settings may be discarded. Read CLAUDE.md, docs/ROADMAP.md, current `config/nginx.conf`/`docker-compose.yml` Octopus co-tenant notes, current ed-new operator docs/workflow, and the upstream Octopus self-hosting files for release v1.0.122. The new host has Docker running the retained V3 stack and approximately 971 GB free; avoid every existing container, network, volume, port, and path. The wrapper will commit and push an isolated branch. Do not commit, push, merge, deploy, access either server, use credentials, change DNS/TLS/GitHub App settings, stop containers, modify databases, or touch product/runtime/Stage 27/V3/CRE/CPE/Journal behavior.

Keep this first slice non-overlapping with the active V3 recovery hardening: add only new Octopus deployment files, focused tests, and a new operations document. Do not edit `.github/workflows/chatgpt-ed-new-ops.yml`, existing operator dispatchers, existing nginx files, or existing Compose files in this slice. A later reviewed integration slice will wire the bundle into an allowlisted operator workflow and public edge.

Required deployment contract:
- pin the Octopus application to upstream release `1.0.122`, never `latest`; use its official self-host image; pin PostgreSQL 17 Alpine and Qdrant 1.17.0 consistently with upstream, preferring immutable digests when they can be verified without guessing;
- use a dedicated `/opt/octopus` target, unique Compose project name, explicit dedicated network, and explicit uniquely named PostgreSQL/Qdrant volumes;
- bind the web service to loopback only on a non-conflicting port (normally `127.0.0.1:43300:3000`) until the public edge is separately approved; PostgreSQL and Qdrant must not be publicly exposed—omit published ports or bind maintenance ports to loopback only;
- default `ENABLE_REVIEW_WORKERS=false` so first boot cannot process GitHub jobs before configuration and cutover; provide an explicit, auditable activation step that changes only this setting after health, login, provider, repository, and webhook checks;
- never use the upstream example password `octopus` in the production bundle. Require strong externally supplied secrets for the Octopus database, `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`, `OCTOPUS_DATA_KEY`, GitHub App credentials, webhook/state secrets, and initial admin access. Commit only placeholders and variable names, never values; set restrictive file-permission expectations;
- use fresh auth/data-encryption secrets because no old encrypted database is being restored. Clearly warn that restoring old data later would require the old `OCTOPUS_DATA_KEY`, but that is outside this fresh-install decision;
- provide deterministic preflight/prepare/migrate/start/health/activate/stop commands or a bounded helper script. Every mutating command must fail closed if `/opt/octopus`, matching containers, volumes, networks, or ports already exist unexpectedly. No `down -v`, broad prune, wildcard removal, or operation capable of touching the V3 stack;
- migrations must run from the exact matching upstream v1.0.122 source/migration set before the application is accepted. Do not assume migrations exist in the runtime image. Design a reproducible method using a pinned checkout and bounded container/tool invocation; no curl-pipe-shell installer;
- include health/version checks (`/api/health`, `/api/version`), container health, PostgreSQL readiness, Qdrant readiness, exact image/version receipts, and a rollback that stops only the fresh Octopus project while retaining its volumes;
- public `octopus.ed-finder.app` DNS/TLS/reverse-proxy cutover and retirement of the old host are explicitly separate later steps. Document that the old Octopus worker/webhook must be stopped before the new worker is activated to prevent duplicate reviews, and that the old server should be destroyed only after at least one controlled exact-head test review succeeds on the new instance.

Provider/credit correctness is mandatory. Current Octopus code applies the internal credit gate unless the organization has its own key for the provider used by the selected review model. Merely setting a process-level provider key can still leave an organization reporting no credits. Document the post-bootstrap UI step to save an organization-level Anthropic BYOK key for the default Claude review path, and an organization-level OpenAI key for default `text-embedding-3-large` indexing, or deliberately configure an alternative local embedding provider. Never include keys. Add a verification checklist proving a test review is classified as BYOK and does not consume platform credits before activating routine reviews.

Add focused tests/contracts that parse the new Compose YAML and helper/docs to prove: exact version pin; no `latest`; loopback-only/public-port absence; review workers disabled by default; strong-secret placeholders with no committed values; unique names; no destructive Docker commands; no references to the old Octopus volumes/data; matching migration version; health/version receipt requirements; BYOK organization-level configuration warning; and no modification of existing V3/ED-Finder deployment authorities. Parse YAML with pinned PyYAML, run relevant repo-hygiene and CI-stall contracts, lint/format changed shell or Python as applicable, and run `git diff --check`. End with exact files, tests/results, operational assumptions, and the one later owner-input checkpoint: provision provider/GitHub App/admin secrets and explicitly authorize the Octopus-unavailable reviewer waiver for the bootstrap integration PR if Octopus cannot review the work intended to restore itself.
